{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1790727081,"nonce":"4MPQm4goRniMw4eaHuSGfbMo5Ug=","title":"Type field-name arrays lose their element type across imported modules","message":"In haxe-conformance TestType, Type.getInstanceFields(MyClass), Type.getClassFields(MySubClass), and Type.getEnumConstructs are lowered as MethodCall expressions with a Dynamic result even though Type.hx declares Array\u003cString\u003e. Their method symbols resolve to Type.getInstanceFields, Type.getClassFields, and Type.getEnumConstructs. Array.join then receives the reference tag and renders raw HaxeString pointers as unknown type IDs; contains can call the uncompiled generic array stub. A cross-module regression project with a class field, kept static field, and enum constructors reproduces the behavior.\n\nThe call result now carries Array\u003cString\u003e when the resolved method's qualified name is one of these three Type APIs. Qualifying by the resolved method avoids changing a user-defined foo.Type.getInstanceFields method. The MIR array tag lookup also resolves aliases for element types. TestType advances past the field assertions, though later unrelated assertions still fail.\n\nValidation: the type_field_names cross-module regression passes, the full authored regression suite passes 92/92, cargo clippy and cargo fmt checks pass. Full scored conformance remains 779/1094 on this run; Issue10083 was the known flaky row that passed in the previous 780/1094 run. TestType is still WRONG_ANSWER due later enum/type-unification assertions, but its field assertions now pass and it no longer aborts at haxe_array_contains.","files":null}]}